Comment:

// @@@ If the index is between 0 and the initial size of the vector inclusive, then for every valid index less than the specified index, the element at that position remains unchanged or is null if it was originally null.

We need to refer to the method add(int index, E element). It calls insertElementAt(element, index). We need to capture initial size (old size). The condition: index between 0 and initial size inclusive. So index >=0 && index <= \old(this.size()).

Then for every valid index i less than specified index, the element at that position remains unchanged or is null if it was originally null.

Interpretation: For each i where 0 <= i < index, after operation, this.elementAt(i) equals the old element (which could be null). So basically this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))? But need to handle nulls: "remains unchanged or is null if it was originally null" means after operation, the element at that position is same as before, which could be null. So equality using == or Objects.equals. In previous examples they used .equals for objects, but need to handle nulls; they used .equals in examples but that would throw NullPointerException if old is null. In spec they sometimes use .equals and assume non-null. But we can express as (this.elementAt(i) == null ? \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null : this.elementAt(i).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))) but that's complex.

Simplify: Use \old(this.elementAt(i)) as old value, and assert that this.elementAt(i) == \old(this.elementAt(i)). This works for null as reference equality. So we can assert equality via ==.

Thus assert: (index >=0 && index <= \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; this.elementAt(i) == \old(this.elementAt(i));

